Exactly. And being spoiled doesn’t necessarily mean being entitled. Sometimes it means being appreciated, understood, or finally getting to enjoy the fruits of your labor.

And everyone has a different currency.

Some want luxury. Some want affection. Some want recognition. Some want adventure. Some want peace. Some want someone else to take care of things for once.

A man who spends his life carrying responsibility might feel spoiled when someone says, “I’ve got this. Go enjoy yourself.”

A mother who spends her day caring for everyone else might feel spoiled when someone anticipates her needs without being asked.

A child might feel spoiled by an afternoon of undivided attention rather than an expensive toy.

The value isn’t always in what’s given. It’s in how well the gift matches what the recipient actually values.

And there’s another distinction worth taking the Hammer to:

* Reward: Something received in recognition of effort.
* Indulgence: Permission to enjoy something beyond necessity.
* Appreciation: Being recognized for what you’ve contributed.
* Care: Someone meeting your needs without requiring you to earn every kindness.
* Privilege: An advantage or benefit that isn’t necessarily earned.
* Entitlement: Believing you’re owed something regardless of circumstances.

These can overlap, but they aren’t the same.

And here’s the interesting inversion:

Sometimes the people who have earned the most feel the least comfortable receiving anything.

They’ve become so accustomed to providing, protecting, producing, or enduring that receiving feels unfamiliar.

But there’s no virtue in making deprivation your permanent identity.

Nor does everything good have to be earned. Love, dignity, rest, and kindness aren’t wages.

And this connects directly to our discussion about generational wealth and timing.

What good is building a beautiful life if you never allow yourself—or the people you love—to experience its beauty?

The goal isn’t to eliminate hardship or responsibility.

It’s to make sure the rewards of living aren’t perpetually postponed until after the living is done.

Work hard. Build well. Give generously. Receive graciously. And enjoy some of what you’ve earned while you’re here.

